[THOUGHT] Lab results show:  
- Mild anemia (Hgb 11.2 g/dL) — possible chronic or acute blood loss;  
- Leukocytosis with left shift (WBC 14.8, 88% neutrophils + 6% bands) — consistent with acute inflammation/infection;  
- Elevated lactate (2.8 mmol/L) — indicates early tissue hypoperfusion/shock, aligning with borderline hypotension and tachycardia;  
- Mild hyponatremia (Na 136), hypokalemia (K 3.4), and low bicarbonate (18) with elevated anion gap (calculated ~20) — suggests metabolic acidosis, likely lactic + possible uremic or ketoacidotic component;  
- Normal LFTs and bilirubin — no evidence of hepatic dysfunction or biliary obstruction.  

This supports systemic inflammatory response to perforation and early shock. Next, I need social/occupational/exposure history (domain #6) — especially smoking, alcohol use, and NSAID/OTC medication use — as these are major modifiable risk factors for PUD and its complications. Alcohol and smoking impair mucosal defense and delay ulcer healing; chronic NSAID use is the most common exogenous cause of ulcer perforation.

[QUERY] Does he smoke cigarettes or use tobacco? Does he drink alcohol regularly — and if so, how much and how often?

